# Golf GTI Mk7.5 — setup sheet.
#
# Edit and save: the car picks it up on the next frame, with no rebuild and without leaving the seat.
# Every line is `key value`. A key nothing is called is an error rather than a line to skip, because a
# misspelling that is silently ignored is a change you made, felt nothing from, and then spent the
# afternoon compensating for somewhere else.
#
# What is *not* here is the car — geometry, masses, the torque curve, the hardpoints. Those come from
# the published data and a setup sheet has no business touching them.
#
# Everything below is commented out, so as shipped this file changes nothing at all and the car is
# exactly what its own data says. Uncomment a line to take it over.

# Springs, N/m along the damper axis (not at the wheel: the motion ratio turns one into the other).
# front.spring    62000
# rear.spring     48000

# Damper rates, N per m/s along the same axis. Stating one of the two leaves the other alone.
# front.bump       4200
# front.rebound    7600
# rear.bump        3800
# rear.rebound     6900

# Anti-roll bars, N/m of differential wheel travel across the axle. Zero disables one.
# front.antiroll  22000
# rear.antiroll   14000

# Peak brake torque per corner, N.m. The front/rear pair is the brake balance.
# front.brake      1400
# rear.brake        900

# The differential. Preload in N.m, ramps as the fraction of input torque each face locks with —
# power on throttle, coast off it. This is the biggest single change to corner exit.
# diff.preload       60
# diff.power_ramp  0.35
# diff.coast_ramp  0.15

# Which way the rack moves for a positive steering demand. It states the *result* rather than being a
# toggle: 1 forces the rack travel negative whatever the car's own data says, 0 forces it positive.
#
# Commented out because this car's data is right. It is here for a car whose is not — and that is not
# hypothetical: until 2026-08-20 the sign was taken from the steering *ratio* rather than from the
# linkage, and this car steered the opposite way to the input on both the wheel and the keyboard.
# steering.invert   0

# Force feedback. Gain is the dial; the ceiling is in N.m at the rim and the gain cannot raise it.
#
# **0.7 is set from the clipping histogram rather than by feel, and it is the knee in that curve.**
# Short clipping is fine — a kerb strike should hit the rail — but *sustained* clipping is the wheel
# going numb through a corner, which is exactly where the tyre's pneumatic trail is collapsing and
# telling you the front is going. Swept over a 261-second session (`ffb-exit.txt` carries the
# histogram every run, which is what it is for):
#
#     gain   clipped   episodes over 250 ms   longest   p95 force
#     1.00    6.38%           17  (9.6 s)      883 ms    8.00 Nm
#     0.85    3.43%           11  (4.7 s)      600 ms    7.33 Nm
#     0.75    1.80%            1  (0.3 s)      267 ms    6.47 Nm
#     0.70    0.95%            0                183 ms    6.04 Nm
#     0.60    0.19%            0                 64 ms    5.17 Nm
#
# Seven tenths is the *highest* gain at which sustained clipping disappears — everything left is
# under 200 ms, which is a bump. Below it there is nothing structural left to buy and lowering it
# only removes force. It costs about 30% of the peak, which is the price of the top of the range
# meaning something.
#
# Check it after any change that moves the tyre, the assist or the ceiling: peak demand should sit
# near 1.3x and clipping near 1%.
#
# **Dominic's, set from the seat 2026-08-21. Do not retune it.**
ffb.gain          0.7
ffb.ceiling       8.0
# Damping opposes the rim's own measured motion, N.m per rad/s — the tuning-menu damper (DPR/NDP)
# this base cannot reach under hid-fanatecff. It is applied after the ceiling *and* after the slew
# limit, because a rate-limited damper arrives late and a velocity term ninety degrees late is a
# spring rather than a damper.
#
# "More of it shook harder" was true and was not the damper being wrong: it was the damper having
# authority at frequencies this loop cannot deliver in phase. Measured on the 12:51 trace, the loop
# carries 12.7 ms of delay end to end, which bounds a damper to about 1/(4*0.0127) = 20 Hz — and an
# unfiltered damper's torque *rises* with frequency, so the oscillation parked itself exactly there.
# Fitting it gives a critical damping of 0.39 N.m per rad/s, against the 0.40 the car itself asks
# for at a standstill. That is the whole of the residual shake.
ffb.damping      0.0

# Hz. The corner of the two-pole low pass on the damper's *measurement* of the rim, which is what
# keeps the damper inside the band the loop's 12.7 ms of delay can actually deliver in. It is not
# smoothing and it is not on the road feel — the car's torque is untouched end to end.
#
# **Four is a sweet spot rather than a compromise, and the table is why.** What matters is not the
# filter's gain but the damper's *effective* damping — its gain times the cosine of the total phase
# lag, filter plus transport — because that is the part that removes energy. Positive removes,
# negative feeds. Measured tau, computed per corner:
#
#     corner    2 Hz (the return to centre)   10 Hz (the residual wobble)
#      2 Hz            -0.08                        -0.03
#      4 Hz            +0.37                        -0.14
#      8 Hz            +0.75                        -0.33
#     none             +0.99                        +0.70   ...but 0.00 at 20 Hz, which is the shake
#
# Unfiltered the damper is fine at ten and dies at twenty — exactly 1/(4*tau) — so the filter's job
# is to be gone by twenty. The cost is that its own phase lag turns the damper mildly negative at
# ten, and four hertz is where that cost is smallest while the two hertz it exists for still gets
# real damping. Below four the return to centre goes negative too and the whole term inverts.
#
# **Eight, not four, since the simulation got its own clock** (2026-08-21, evening). The table above
# is still the right method and its numbers were taken at a loop this engine no longer runs. Four was
# never the damper's best corner — it was the largest one that did not shake, and eight is worth
# twice as much return to centre (+0.75 against +0.37). What made eight shake was not the damper
# alone: a limit cycle needs an exciter as well as a negative term, and the exciter was the road-feel
# path chopping at the frame rate, which a fixed-rate simulation thread removed. With nothing feeding
# it, the mildly negative term at ten hertz has nothing to amplify.
#
# **Dominic's, chosen from the seat 2026-08-21 after a session at several values. Do not retune it.**
ffb.damperhz      8.0

# The pedal motors' cue: where it starts and where it saturates, as multiples of the tyre's own peak
# slip — so a compound change moves the cue with the grip. Stage one answers "how far past its limit
# is the tyre under this foot"; these set the range the motor renders that over, and smaller numbers
# are a more sensitive pedal.
#
# onset is deliberately past the peak (1.0 *is* the peak): a cue at the peak buzzes on every perfect
# threshold stop and teaches braking less hard. The two full points differ because slip is bounded
# at -1 under braking and unbounded under power — a lit-up launch reads twenty peaks, a scrabble off
# a damp junction two or three.
#
# The model's own defaults are onset 1.15, brake_full 2.75, throttle_full 12. The two active lines
# start at roughly half those ranges — "they could be more sensitive", first bench test 2026-08-21 —
# and they are feel numbers to move from the seat, exactly as ffb.gain was.
# pedal.onset          1.15
pedal.brake_full     2.0
pedal.throttle_full  6.0

# --- the electronics -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ABS, traction control and XDS. Commented out, which means **off** — and off is not what the real
# car does, so this is a deliberate default rather than an oversight. Every measurement this project
# has of this car is of the car underneath the electronics: both parity goldens, the 0-100, the
# skidpad, the coastdown. Switching them on here changes what the driving gate captures, so a sheet
# with these lines live wants a golden re-bless the same way a spring change does.
#
# Driven and accepted 2026-08-23 with all three on -- "drives well, makes a massive difference".
#
# What each is worth, measured (docs/abs-and-traction-control-brief.md):
#
#   abs   On dry tarmac, almost nothing: this car cannot lock its front wheels at any pedal
#         position, because brakes.ini's 4200 N.m caps it at 0.997 g. It is worth 22% and three
#         quarters of the yaw on a split surface, and it keeps the steering under a full stop.
#   tc    'off', 'full' or 'sport'. Full holds 10% wheel slip and takes 0-100 from 6.550 s to
#         6.022 s; sport holds 22%, which is 6.153 s and a car that will rotate on the way out of a
#         corner. On a slippery surface full is the difference between reaching 100 km/h and not.
#   xds   Brakes the inside front while cornering to stand in for a limited-slip differential. It is
#         a slight application by design -- a seventh of full braking on one wheel.
#
# OSR_ASSISTS overrides all three for one session without touching this file: a comma-separated list
# of abs, tc, tc-sport and xds, or the single word 'none'. That is what the gates use, so editing
# this sheet cannot move a golden by accident.

assist.abs  1
assist.tc   sport
#assist.xds  1
